  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 239 AM EDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nAs the upper low overhead slowly slides southeastward through the \nday today into tonight, shallow moisture depths below 700 mb finally \ndry out below 850 mb after 21z. This is as dry air advects eastward \neffectively removing drizzle chances and allowing overcast to break \nup. This also helps gusts to relax for Thursday and Friday. 850\nmb temperatures slowly rise from the upper single digits into \nthe low teens as early as Thursday morning, but mixing heights \nstruggle to reach 850 mb until Thursday so upper 60s to perhaps \naround 70 degree highs still look likely. This fight between \nlapse rates, 850 mb temps between the upper single digits and \nlow teens, and times of negative theta-e advection continue \nthrough the weekend. During this time, our upper low shifts \ntowards the east coast and a rex block sets up across the \neastern Seaboard, effectively directing upper flow north of the \narea on Saturday and keeping the area in mid level ridging at \nleast until we get to midweek next week. As a result, it is \npossible that our highs on Monday and Tuesday are both too warm \nand we maintain the upper 60s to around 70 then, therefore \nwaiting until Tuesday or Wednesday for getting securely back \ninto the 70s.",
